1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,680
Issuers, wallets and verifiers from different vendors
are supposed to work together.

2
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:10,760
Interop Playground checks whether they actually do,
and when they don't, it shows you why.

3
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:14,680
It starts with one command.

4
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:21,260
That brings up the full Inji stack, Certify, Verify,
Mimoto and eSignet, on your own machine,

5
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:23,720
with every version pinned.

6
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:30,300
First, you pick who plays each role:
an issuer, a wallet and a verifier.

7
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:35,980
If a combination can't work, the playground tells you why
before you even run it.

8
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:43,880
Press Run, and the credential is issued
over OpenID for Verifiable Credential Issuance.

9
00:00:43,930 --> 00:00:48,400
Every arrow in the diagram
is a real HTTP message we captured.

10
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:53,140
Here's the credential offer, the access token,
and the credential itself.

11
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:59,840
Now the wallet holds the credential.

12
00:00:59,890 --> 00:01:05,260
The inspector shows exactly what's inside: who signed it,
which fields can be selectively disclosed,

13
00:01:05,310 --> 00:01:08,520
and the key that binds it to the holder.

14
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:13,260
Next, Inji Verify asks the wallet for proof.

15
00:01:13,310 --> 00:01:17,960
The wallet presents the credential, signed with its own key,
and Verify checks it.

16
00:01:18,010 --> 00:01:21,180
The answer comes back: valid.

17
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:30,200
Every run ends with a report: who took part,
which format was used, and the result.

18
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:33,740
If something failed, it points to the exact check.

19
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:39,480
The playground can tell valid, invalid and expired apart.

20
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:43,600
Here the signature is fine,
but the credential has expired.

21
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:49,300
Now the interesting part.

22
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:56,180
We swap in an issuer from outside the Inji ecosystem,
walt.id, and verification fails.

23
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:57,880
The playground recognises this failure:

24
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:05,340
Inji Verify expects the issuer's certificate chain
in the credential, and walt.id doesn't send it.

25
00:02:05,390 --> 00:02:07,300
It suggests one change.

26
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:11,220
One click re-runs with only that change,
and now it passes.

27
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:14,800
So this isn't a guess. It's a proven fix.

28
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:26,980
All of this adds up to an interop report:
every combination we tested and how it went,

29
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:31,920
plus a write-up of the Inji issues we found,
each with steps to reproduce.

30
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:36,200
Interop Playground, by Team Bhrinjal.

31
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:40,140
The code, the setup and every flow are on GitHub.
